//! core/net/server — being a server.
//!
//! `core/net/http` is the client half: `http.send(ctx, request)` makes one
//! request and answers one response. This is the other half — a program that
//! *is* a server. Two modules because two authorities: `Net` means the program
//! talks to servers, `Listen` means it accepts connections, and `Sockets` means
//! it can push on one somebody else accepted.
//!
//! **The same two types.** `Request` and `Response` are `core/effect`'s, the
//! ones `core/net/http` re-exports, so a handler here answers with the same
//! `Response` a client reads and builds it with the same
//! `http.text` / `http.status` / `withHeader`.
//!
//! ```buri
//! from "core/effect" import { Alloc, Listen, Tasks };
//! from "core/host" import * as host;
//! from "core/net/http" import * as http;
//! from "core/net/server" import * as server;
//!
//! export fn main(): Result<(), Str> {
//!     let ctx = context {
//!         Alloc: host.alloc,
//!         Listen: host.listen,
//!         Tasks: host.tasks,
//!     };
//!     server
//!         .serve(ctx, server.Server {
//!             port: 3000,
//!             onRequest: fn(c, request) => {
//!                 match (request.path()) {
//!                     "/ping" => http.text(c, "pong"),
//!                     _ => http.status(404),
//!                 }
//!             },
//!         })
//!         .mapErr(server.errorText)
//! }
//! ```
//!
//! ## Every knob is a field, and an omitted one is `.None`
//!
//! `Server` carries the whole configuration — address, protocols, limits and
//! deadlines — and every field but `port` and `onRequest` is an `Option`. A
//! literal may leave an `Option` field out and the checker fills in `.None`
//! (SPEC 5.6), which is why the example above is three lines long.
//!
//! **`.None` is not "the default". It is the absence of a choice, and the
//! runtime turns that absence into a default.** Writing nothing commits this
//! module to no number, so a toolchain whose runtime picks differently is not
//! breaking the contract. A program that wants a number pinned writes it.
//!
//! `bind` renders the `.None`s into what `effect Listen` calls *unset*, and
//! this is the whole of the encoding:
//!
//! | Field | `.None` crosses as | And the acceptor today |
//! |---|---|---|
//! | `address` | `""` | listens on `127.0.0.1` |
//! | `protocols` | no `.Speak` in the plan | speaks HTTP/1.1 |
//! | `tls` | no `.Certificate` in the plan | serves cleartext |
//! | `requestLimit` | `-1` | answers until the listener is closed |
//! | `idleTimeoutMillis` | `-1` | waits for a connection indefinitely |
//! | `drainMillis` | no `.DrainMillis` in the plan | waits ten seconds for the requests in flight |
//! | `websocket` | nothing at all — the plan never mentions it | never upgrades, so an upgrade request is an ordinary one |
//! | `socketBuffer` | no `.SocketBuffer` in the plan | holds sixty-four messages per socket |
//!
//! The third column is what *this* runtime does. It is here so a reader knows
//! what to expect, not so a program may rely on it. The second column is the
//! contract.
//!
//! **`protocols` and `tls` cross as one list**, which `effect Listen` calls the
//! plan and `bind` builds. A list crosses as a pointer and a length however many
//! things are in it, so the fields a `Server` grows from now on are variants of
//! `Serve` rather than arguments nobody has room for.
//!
//! **How many handlers run at once is not a `Server` field**, because it is an
//! *answer* rather than a setting. The acceptor says how many it will host on
//! `Listener.handlers`, and `run` fans out to that number. A program that has
//! to bound its own concurrency bounds it inside its handler.
//!
//! ## A handler runs on a task, and that is why `Tasks` is in the bound
//!
//! `run` does not drive one accept loop. It fans out `listener.handlers` of
//! them with `Tasks.parallel`, each on a carrier of its own, all accepting on
//! the one listener. A slow handler therefore holds up its own connection and
//! nothing else, which is what "many connections in flight" means here.
//!
//! **A worker pool and not a task per request.** `Tasks.parallel` has no
//! detached spawn — every task finishes before the call returns — so the loop
//! that accepts is the loop that answers, and running several of those is how
//! they overlap. The concurrency is bounded, and the bound is
//! `Listener.handlers`.
//!
//! A program that grants `Listen` and not `Tasks` is not a slower server. It
//! does not compile, and the message names what is missing.
//!
//! ## TLS, and the protocol ALPN chooses
//!
//! A `Server` with a `tls` serves HTTPS, and the same handler answers both
//! ways. A handler never learns which transport its request arrived over,
//! because nothing about a `Request` depends on it.
//!
//! **HTTP/2 comes with TLS and only with TLS.** ALPN chooses it inside the
//! handshake (RFC 7540 §3.3), so a `Server` asking for `.Http2` without a
//! certificate gets `.Err(.Unsupported)` from `bind` with a message saying so,
//! rather than being quietly served in HTTP/1.1. A `Server` with a certificate
//! and no `protocols` offers HTTP/1.1: growing a certificate is not a program
//! asking for a second protocol.
//!
//! ```buri
//! from "core/effect" import { Alloc, Listen, Tasks };
//! from "core/host" import * as host;
//! from "core/net/http" import * as http;
//! from "core/net/server" import * as server;
//!
//! export fn main(): Result<(), Str> {
//!     let ctx = context {
//!         Alloc: host.alloc,
//!         Listen: host.listen,
//!         Tasks: host.tasks,
//!     };
//!     server
//!         .serve(ctx, server.Server {
//!             port: 8443,
//!             protocols: .Some([.Http1, .Http2]),
//!             tls: .Some(server.Tls {
//!                 certificate: "/etc/ssl/example.pem",
//!                 key: "/etc/ssl/example.key",
//!             }),
//!             onRequest: fn(c, _request) => http.text(c, "hello"),
//!         })
//!         .mapErr(server.errorText)
//! }
//! ```
//!
//! ## Stopping, and what `.Ok(())` means
//!
//! `serve` answers `.Ok(())` when the server stopped **the way it was asked
//! to**, and there are three ways to ask. Two are fields: a `requestLimit` that
//! is spent, and a listener the program closed. The third is the operating
//! system's — a `SIGTERM` from a supervisor, or a `SIGINT` from somebody at a
//! terminal — and on a platform that grants `Listen` it does not kill the
//! process.
//!
//! What it does instead is a **drain**, in three steps:
//!
//! 1. the platform stops accepting connections;
//! 2. the workers holding the requests already in flight answer them and finish
//!    normally;
//! 3. `listenAccept` answers `.Closed`, every worker ends, the port goes back,
//!    and `serve` returns `.Ok(())`.
//!
//! So a `main` that ends with `serve(…)` falls off its own end when something
//! asks it to stop, and whatever the program does after `serve` — flush a log,
//! close a database, print a line — happens.
//!
//! `drainMillis` is how long step 2 may take. It bounds the *waiting*, not the
//! handlers: a handler still running when it expires still answers the request
//! it was given. What runs out is the patience of the workers with nothing left
//! to do.
//!
//! **A second signal is not a second drain.** The platform hands the signal
//! back to the operating system when it catches the first one, so a second
//! `Ctrl-C` during a slow shutdown terminates the process the ordinary way.
//!
//! The signals are the platform's only while a listener is open, so a program
//! that is not a server keeps the operating system's own behaviour.
//!
//! ## What is here, and what is not yet
//!
//! One request per HTTP/1.1 connection: the response says `connection: close`
//! and the socket goes away after it. Several requests share an HTTP/2
//! connection — that is what multiplexing is — and a worker answers each of
//! them exactly as it answers an HTTP/1.1 one. Keep-alive, meaning several
//! requests on one *HTTP/1.1* connection, is still not here.
//!
//! ## WebSockets
//!
//! **The upgrade is invisible.** A `Server` with a `websocket` accepts upgrade
//! requests at the path its hooks name and runs `onOpen`. A `Server` without
//! one does not, and the same request reaches `onRequest` like any other. There
//! is no branch in a handler and no "accept the upgrade" call.
//!
//! **The hooks say where they are served, and `WebSocket.path` is required.** A
//! request to any other path is an ordinary request even when it carries every
//! RFC 6455 header. The match is exact: it ignores the query string and
//! normalises nothing, so `"/socket"` and `"/socket/"` are two different
//! paths.
//!
//! ```buri
//! from "core/actor" import * as actor;
//! from "core/actor" import { Actor, Stepped };
//! from "core/effect" import { Alloc, Listen, Sockets, Tasks };
//! from "core/host" import * as host;
//! from "core/net/http" import * as http;
//! from "core/net/server" import * as server;
//! from "core/str" import * as str;
//!
//! enum Counting {
//!     Increment,
//! }
//!
//! fn counter<C: Alloc + Tasks>(): Actor<C, Int, Counting, Int> {
//!     Actor {
//!         state: 0,
//!         step: fn(c, count, message) => {
//!             match (message) {
//!                 .Increment => Stepped { state: count + 1, answer: count + 1 },
//!             }
//!         },
//!     }
//! }
//!
//! export fn main(): Result<(), Str> {
//!     let ctx = context {
//!         Alloc: host.alloc,
//!         Listen: host.listen,
//!         Sockets: host.sockets,
//!         Tasks: host.tasks,
//!     };
//!     server
//!         .serve(ctx, server.Server {
//!             port: 3000,
//!             onRequest: fn(_c, _request) => http.status(404),
//!             websocket: .Some(server.WebSocket {
//!                 path: "/socket",
//!                 onOpen: fn(c, _socket, _request) => actor.start(c, counter()),
//!                 onMessage: fn(c, socket, counted, _message) => {
//!                     let count = counted.sendMessage(c, .Increment).withDefault(0);
//!                     let said = str.format(c, "messages so far: ${count}");
//!                     let _sent = socket.send(c, .Text(said));
//!                     counted
//!                 },
//!                 onClose: fn(c, _socket, counted, _reason) => {
//!                     let _stopped = counted.stop(c);
//!                     ()
//!                 },
//!             }),
//!         })
//!         .mapErr(server.errorText)
//!     // The socket's state is its counter's address.
//! }
//! ```
//!
//! * **Every other path is the handler's.** The example answers `404` from
//!   `onRequest`, and that is what a client asking to upgrade `/` is told —
//!   the same answer it would get from a server with no `websocket` at all.
//! * **`onOpen` decides what a socket carries.** Every later hook is handed the
//!   current one, and `onMessage` answers the next — so per-socket state is a
//!   value on a stack rather than a table keyed by socket. Above it is an
//!   actor's address; it could as easily be a room name or a count.
//! * **`Socket` is inert**: one integer, copyable, comparable, and sendable to
//!   an actor, which can then push on it long after the request that opened it
//!   returned. `socket.send` and `socket.close` need `C: Sockets` and nothing
//!   else.
//! * **`send` never waits.** It hands the message to the socket's outbound
//!   buffer. A buffer that fills closes the socket with `.Overflow` and runs
//!   `onClose`. The platform drops a message to a socket that has already
//!   closed.
//! * **A close is a `CloseReason` and never a wire code**, in both directions.
//! * **Ping and pong are the platform's.** `onMessage` sees `.Text` and
//!   `.Binary` and nothing else.
//!
//! **A socket costs a worker.** Its whole life — `onOpen`, every `onMessage`,
//! `onClose` — runs on the one worker that accepted it, so the hooks on one
//! socket run in order by construction rather than by a lock. The bill: a
//! server holding `listener.handlers` sockets has no worker left to accept
//! with.
//!
//! A shutdown closes any open socket with `.GoingAway` — the WebSocket half of
//! the GOAWAY an HTTP/2 connection gets — so a drain does not wait out its
//! deadline for a client that is doing nothing wrong. `onClose` still runs.

from "core/effect" import {
    Alloc, Listen, Listener, Protocol, Request, Response, Serve, ServeError,
    ServeFailure, Sockets, Tasks,
};
from "core/list" import * as list;
from "core/tasks" import * as tasks;

from "core/effect" export {
    Listener, Protocol, Request, Response, Serve, ServeError, ServeFailure,
};

/// A server, whole.
///
/// `port` and `onRequest` are the two a server cannot be without: a port to
/// hold, and something to answer with. Everything else is an `Option` a literal
/// may leave out, and `.None` means "the runtime chooses".
///
/// `C` is the caller's own context, and `onRequest` is handed it. A handler may
/// therefore do anything the context it was written beside can do: allocate a
/// response body, print a line, read a clock, start a task. None of that
/// authority passes through `Listen`, because `run`'s workers call the handler
/// themselves.
///
/// `S` is **the state one socket carries**. On a server with no `websocket`
/// nothing constrains it and the checker settles it as `()`, so a program that
/// is not a WebSocket server never spells the parameter at all.
export struct Server<C, S> {
    /// The port to hold. `0` asks the operating system to choose one, and
    /// `bind` answers with the number it chose.
    export port: Int,
    /// What answers a request. Called once per request, on one of `run`'s
    /// worker tasks, with the context `serve` was given.
    export onRequest: fn(C, Request) => Response,
    /// The interface to listen on. `.None` lets the runtime choose, which on
    /// every platform that grants `Listen` today means loopback — a server
    /// that wants to be reachable from another machine says so.
    export address: Option<Str>,
    /// The HTTP versions to speak. `.None` is HTTP/1.1. Asking for a protocol
    /// this toolchain's runtime was not built with gets `.Err(.Unsupported)`
    /// from `bind`, with a message naming the switch that would add it. So does
    /// `.Http2` without a `tls` — see that field.
    export protocols: Option<[Protocol]>,
    /// Stop after answering this many requests, and return `.Ok(())`. `.None`
    /// serves until the listener closes.
    ///
    /// It is a knob, not a debugging aid: a one-shot callback receiver, an OAuth
    /// redirect catcher and a test are all servers that finish.
    export requestLimit: Option<Int>,
    /// Give up waiting for a connection after this long, and return
    /// `.Err(.Timeout)`. `.None` waits indefinitely, which is what an ordinary
    /// server does.
    export idleTimeoutMillis: Option<Int>,
    /// How long a shutdown may wait for the requests already in flight.
    /// `.None` leaves the number to the runtime.
    ///
    /// A deployment sets this one: a supervisor that sends `SIGTERM` and then
    /// `SIGKILL` ten seconds later is asking a question this field answers. It
    /// bounds the middle step of the drain the module header writes out.
    export drainMillis: Option<Int>,
    /// The certificate this server presents, and therefore whether it speaks
    /// TLS at all. `.None` is a cleartext server.
    ///
    /// It also decides whether `.Http2` is reachable. ALPN chooses HTTP/2
    /// inside the TLS handshake (RFC 7540 §3.3), so `bind` refuses a server
    /// that asks for it without a certificate rather than quietly serving
    /// HTTP/1.1.
    export tls: Option<Tls>,
    /// What this server does with a WebSocket, and the path it does it at.
    ///
    /// **The upgrade is invisible.** There is no "accept the upgrade" call and
    /// no branch in a handler. With hooks present, a client that asks for a
    /// socket *at `WebSocket.path`* gets one and `onOpen` runs. Without them —
    /// or at any other path — the request reaches `onRequest` like any other.
    export websocket: Option<WebSocket<C, S>>,
    /// How many messages one socket's outbound buffer holds before the socket
    /// closes with `.Overflow`. `.None` leaves the number to the runtime.
    ///
    /// It is a `Server` field rather than a `WebSocket` one because `bind` tells
    /// the acceptor once, when the port opens, and every socket on the listener
    /// gets the same bound.
    export socketBuffer: Option<Int>,
}

derive Eq, Show for Tls;
/// Where a server reads its identity from.
///
/// **Two paths and not two PEM documents**: certificates expire and get
/// rotated, and a key written into a source file is a key in version control. So
/// a program names where to read them, and the runtime reads them once, when the
/// port opens. A certificate that is missing, unreadable, or does not match its
/// key is an `.Err` from `bind` rather than a handshake failure later.
///
/// Both fields are required.
export struct Tls {
    /// The PEM file holding the certificate chain, leaf first.
    export certificate: Str,
    /// The PEM file holding the private key that goes with the leaf. The
    /// runtime reads PKCS#8, PKCS#1 and SEC1; the label in the file says
    /// which.
    export key: Str,
}

//
// Two knobs a reader might look for are absent: **how long one connection may
// take to send its head**, and **the largest body the acceptor will read**.
// Both are the runtime's own numbers. The day a program needs to name one, it
// becomes a variant of `Serve` and a field here.

/// The whole of a server: bind the port, answer requests until the listener is
/// done, give the port back.
///
/// `.Ok(())` means the server stopped the way it was asked to — its request
/// limit spent, its listener closed, or the platform asked it to stop and it
/// finished draining. An error is a reason it could not go on, and it gives the
/// port back on that path too.
///
/// A program that needs the port *while* the server runs — anything binding
/// `port: 0` — wants this function's two halves, `bind` and `run`.
export fn serve<C: Alloc + Listen + Tasks, S>(
    ctx: C,
    server: Server<C, S>,
): Result<(), ServeError> {
    match (bind(ctx, server)) {
        .Err(e) => .Err(e),
        .Ok(listener) => run(ctx, listener, server),
    }
}

/// Opens the port and answers with it, without serving anything yet.
///
/// The point of the split is `port: 0`: the operating system chooses, and a
/// program that has to publish the number — write it to a file, print it, hand
/// it to a task that will connect — reads `listener.port` here, between the
/// bind and the loop.
///
/// **`Alloc` is in the bound because the plan is a list.** `listenBind` takes
/// one `[Serve]`, and building it allocates.
export fn bind<C: Alloc + Listen, S>(
    ctx: C,
    server: Server<C, S>,
): Result<Listener, ServeError> {
    ctx.listenBind(
        server.address.withDefault(""),
        server.port,
        planned(ctx, server),
        server.requestLimit.withDefault(-1),
        server.idleTimeoutMillis.withDefault(-1),
    )
}

/// A `Server`'s protocol and TLS fields, rendered into the one list the bind
/// takes.
///
/// Protocols first, then the identity, then the shutdown, then the socket
/// buffer — the order the acceptor reads them in.
///
/// **An absent option is an absent element.** There is no "unset"
/// `.DrainMillis` to render, because a plan that does not mention the drain
/// already says the program chose none. An empty list is a caller who chose
/// nothing at all.
fn planned<C: Alloc, S>(ctx: C, server: Server<C, S>): [Serve] {
    let spoken: [Serve] = server.protocols.withDefault([]).map(ctx, fn(p) => .Speak(p));
    let secured: [Serve] = match (server.tls) {
        .None => spoken,
        .Some(tls) => {
            spoken.concat(ctx, [.Certificate(tls.certificate), .PrivateKey(tls.key)])
        },
    };
    let drained: [Serve] = match (server.drainMillis) {
        .None => secured,
        .Some(millis) => secured.concat(ctx, [.DrainMillis(millis)]),
    };
    match (server.socketBuffer) {
        .None => drained,
        .Some(messages) => drained.concat(ctx, [.SocketBuffer(messages)]),
    }
}

/// The most workers this module will start, whatever a `Listener` says.
///
/// A `Listener` is inert data and a program may write one for itself, so `run`
/// believes its `handlers` only inside a range it can host: sixty-four, which is
/// the runtime's own ceiling too. The clamp is here because this module cannot
/// check what minted the value.
let MAX_HANDLERS: Int = 64;

/// `asked`, believed inside the range above. At least one: a server with no
/// workers is a port nobody is listening on.
fn hosted(asked: Int): Int {
    if (asked < 1) {
        1
    } else {
        if (asked > MAX_HANDLERS) { MAX_HANDLERS } else { asked }
    }
}

/// One worker's whole job: which listener to answer on, and what to answer
/// with.
///
/// It exists so the fan-out's closure captures **nothing**. A task gets its item
/// and its context and takes nothing from the frame it was written in (SPEC
/// 10.6), so what a worker needs travels as the item.
struct Worker<C, S> {
    handle: Int,
    onRequest: fn(C, Request) => Response,
    websocket: Option<WebSocket<C, S>>,
}

/// Answers requests on an already-open listener until it is done, then closes
/// it.
///
/// **`listener.handlers` workers, each running the whole accept loop.** They all
/// accept on the one listener and the acceptor hands each connection to
/// whichever asks first, so a handler that takes a second holds up its own
/// connection and no other. The loop is `answering` below, and it is a self tail
/// call, so each worker costs one frame however long it runs.
///
/// **One handler is no fan-out at all**: the loop runs on the calling task, with
/// no task started, no list built and no result folded.
///
/// It takes the whole `Server` rather than its `onRequest`, and ignores the
/// fields `bind` already spent.
///
/// The answer is the **first** failure any worker reported, and `.Ok(())` when
/// every one of them ended the ordinary way. A worker that fails closes the
/// listener on its way out (see `answering`), so the others are not left waiting
/// out a server that has already stopped.
export fn run<C: Alloc + Listen + Tasks, S>(
    ctx: C,
    listener: Listener,
    server: Server<C, S>,
): Result<(), ServeError> {
    let outcome = if (hosted(listener.handlers) <= 1) {
        answering(ctx, listener.handle, server.onRequest, server.websocket)
    } else {
        together(ctx, listener, server)
    };
    let _closed = ctx.listenClose(listener.handle);
    outcome
}

/// The workers, fanned out, and the first failure any of them reported.
fn together<C: Alloc + Listen + Tasks, S>(
    ctx: C,
    listener: Listener,
    server: Server<C, S>,
): Result<(), ServeError> {
    let worker = Worker {
        handle: listener.handle,
        onRequest: server.onRequest,
        websocket: server.websocket,
    };
    let outcomes = tasks.parallel(
        ctx,
        list.repeat(ctx, worker, hosted(listener.handlers)),
        fn(c, _index, w) => answering(c, w.handle, w.onRequest, w.websocket),
    );
    outcomes.fold(
        fn(sofar, outcome) => {
            match (sofar) {
                .Err(e) => .Err(e),
                .Ok(_ok) => outcome,
            }
        },
        .Ok(()),
    )
}

/// One request, then the rest of them.
///
/// Four calls per turn: accept a connection, read its request, answer it, go
/// round. Only the first of them waits.
///
/// The third is `taking`, where a connection becomes a socket if the client
/// asked for one. A socket runs to its close on this worker and then the loop
/// goes round, so `.Ok(())` from `taking` means the connection is finished with
/// whichever kind it was.
///
/// `.Closed` is the ordinary end and becomes `.Ok(())` here, so no caller of
/// this module has to know that a listener signs off by failing. It is read from
/// **either** call, because an acceptor may notice it is done when it takes the
/// last response as easily as when it is next asked for a request.
fn answering<C: Listen, S>(
    ctx: C,
    handle: Int,
    onRequest: fn(C, Request) => Response,
    websocket: Option<WebSocket<C, S>>,
): Result<(), ServeError> {
    match (ctx.listenAccept(handle)) {
        .Err(e) => ending(ctx, handle, e),
        .Ok(connection) => {
            match (ctx.listenRequest(connection)) {
                .Err(e) => ending(ctx, handle, e),
                .Ok(request) => {
                    match (taking(ctx, connection, request, onRequest, websocket)) {
                        .Err(e) => ending(ctx, handle, e),
                        .Ok(_ok) => answering(ctx, handle, onRequest, websocket),
                    }
                },
            }
        },
    }
}

/// One connection, once its request has been read: a socket if the client
/// asked for one at the path the hooks name, and an ordinary request otherwise.
///
/// **Every way the upgrade does not happen ends in `onRequest`.** A server with
/// no hooks never asks; a server whose hooks are served at another path never
/// asks either, because this compares the path first; and a server that does ask
/// gets `.Err` for every request that was not an upgrade. All three leave the
/// connection untouched.
///
/// **This compares the path in Buri, not in the runtime.** `listenUpgrade` takes
/// a connection and nothing else, so a routing rule never sits behind the C
/// ABI.
fn taking<C: Listen, S>(
    ctx: C,
    connection: Int,
    request: Request,
    onRequest: fn(C, Request) => Response,
    websocket: Option<WebSocket<C, S>>,
): Result<(), ServeError> {
    match (websocket) {
        .None => answered(ctx, connection, request, onRequest),
        .Some(hooks) => {
            if (request.path() == hooks.path) {
                match (ctx.listenUpgrade(connection)) {
                    .Err(_notAnUpgrade) => {
                        answered(ctx, connection, request, onRequest)
                    },
                    .Ok(socket) => opened(ctx, hooks, Socket(socket), request),
                }
            } else {
                answered(ctx, connection, request, onRequest)
            }
        },
    }
}

/// The handler, and its answer on the connection it was asked about.
fn answered<C: Listen>(
    ctx: C,
    connection: Int,
    request: Request,
    onRequest: fn(C, Request) => Response,
): Result<(), ServeError> {
    let handler = onRequest;
    let reply = handler(ctx, request);
    ctx.listenRespond(connection, reply.status, reply.headers, reply.body)
}

/// A socket, from the hook that opens it to the hook that closes it.
///
/// **The socket's whole life runs on the worker that accepted it.** The state is
/// a local of this frame, threaded through `pumping`'s self tail call. A socket
/// occupies one of `listener.handlers` workers until it closes, so a server
/// holding more sockets than it has handlers has no worker left to accept with.
///
/// The answer is `.Ok(())` however the socket ended. A socket closing is not a
/// listener failing, and the worker goes back to accepting.
fn opened<C: Listen, S>(
    ctx: C,
    hooks: WebSocket<C, S>,
    socket: Socket,
    request: Request,
): Result<(), ServeError> {
    let open = hooks.onOpen;
    let state = open(ctx, socket, request);
    let _closed = pumping(ctx, hooks, socket, state);
    .Ok(())
}

/// One message, then the next, then the close.
///
/// A self tail call, so a socket that carries a million messages costs one
/// frame.
///
/// **`.Err` is a close too.** A socket that is already gone when this asks — its
/// listener closed under it, or a handle spent twice — reaches `onClose` as
/// `.Abnormal` rather than getting dropped, so a program always gets to undo
/// what `onOpen` did.
fn pumping<C: Listen, S>(ctx: C, hooks: WebSocket<C, S>, socket: Socket, state: S): () {
    match (ctx.listenReceive(socket.0)) {
        .Err(_gone) => closed(ctx, hooks, socket, state, .Abnormal),
        .Ok(received) => {
            match (received.frame) {
                .Closed => closed(ctx, hooks, socket, state, reasonOf(received.code)),
                .Text => {
                    let step = hooks.onMessage;
                    pumping(
                        ctx,
                        hooks,
                        socket,
                        step(ctx, socket, state, .Text(received.text)),
                    )
                },
                .Binary => {
                    let step = hooks.onMessage;
                    pumping(
                        ctx,
                        hooks,
                        socket,
                        step(ctx, socket, state, .Binary(received.data)),
                    )
                },
            }
        },
    }
}

/// The close hook, run once, with the state the last message left.
fn closed<C: Listen, S>(
    ctx: C,
    hooks: WebSocket<C, S>,
    socket: Socket,
    state: S,
    reason: CloseReason,
): () {
    let close = hooks.onClose;
    close(ctx, socket, state, reason)
}

/// How a worker ends: the ordinary way, or by taking the listener down with it.
///
/// `.Closed` is the ordinary end and becomes `.Ok(())`. Anything else is a
/// reason this worker cannot go on, and **it closes the listener before
/// reporting that**, because the other workers are blocked in an accept nothing
/// else would interrupt. Closing is idempotent, so every worker doing it is one
/// close and n - 1 no-ops.
fn ending<C: Listen>(ctx: C, handle: Int, e: ServeError): Result<(), ServeError> {
    match (e.cause) {
        .Closed => .Ok(()),
        _ => {
            let _closed = ctx.listenClose(handle);
            .Err(e)
        },
    }
}

/// A `ServeError`'s cause as one line of English, for a `main` that answers
/// `Result<(), Str>`.
///
/// A free function rather than a method, because `core/effect` declares
/// `ServeError` and a method must live in its type's defining module.
///
/// The **cause only**, and it costs no context: each answer is a constant per
/// variant, so this needs no `Alloc` and works from anywhere. `e.detail` holds
/// the platform's own words, and a program that wants both writes
/// `"${server.errorText(e)}: ${e.detail}"`.
export fn errorText(e: ServeError): Str {
    match (e.cause) {
        .AddressInUse => "the address is already in use",
        .AddressNotAvailable => "the address is not available on this machine",
        .PermissionDenied => "listening was not permitted",
        .Unsupported => "the protocol is not supported by this toolchain",
        .Timeout => "no connection arrived before the deadline",
        .Closed => "the listener is closed",
        .Transport => "the transport failed",
    }
}

// --- Sockets ------------------------------------------------------------------

derive Eq, Show for Socket;
/// An open WebSocket, as a value a program can hold.
///
/// **Inert, and deliberately so.** It is one integer the acceptor minted and
/// nobody else understands, so a program may copy it, put it in a list, compare
/// it, and *send it to an actor*.
///
/// Its two methods need `C: Sockets` and nothing else, which lets an actor that
/// was handed a socket push on it long after the request that opened it
/// returned. Reading from a socket is not a `Socket` method: that belongs to
/// whoever holds the listener.
export struct Socket(export Int);

derive Eq, Show for Message;
/// One WebSocket message.
///
/// Two variants and not four: a ping and a pong are the transport keeping a
/// connection alive, and the platform answers them.
export enum Message {
    /// A UTF-8 message. The wire's text frame.
    Text(Str),
    /// An octet message. The wire's binary frame.
    Binary([U8]),
}

derive Eq, Show for CloseReason;
/// Why a socket closed.
///
/// **An enum and never a wire code.** `codeOf` and `reasonOf` are the two halves
/// of the mapping, and they are the only places in this language anyone spells a
/// close code.
///
/// Seven of the nine are RFC 6455's. Two are not, and both are things a socket
/// is *told* rather than things a program says.
export enum CloseReason {
    /// The exchange finished. Code 1000, and what a program means by "close
    /// this" unless it says otherwise.
    Normal,
    /// One side is going away — a page navigating, a server draining. Code
    /// 1001, and it is what a shutdown closes an open socket with.
    GoingAway,
    /// The far side broke the protocol. Code 1002.
    ProtocolError,
    /// A message this end cannot accept — the wrong framing for what it does.
    /// Code 1003.
    Unsupported,
    /// A message this end will not accept, for a reason of its own. Code 1008.
    Policy,
    /// A message larger than this end will read. Code 1009.
    TooLarge,
    /// This end broke. Code 1011.
    InternalError,
    /// **The outbound buffer filled.** `send` never waits, so a client that
    /// reads more slowly than this server writes has to end somewhere, and the
    /// end is here: the socket closes and `onClose` gets told this.
    ///
    /// It is not a wire code and no peer can forge it — the platform says it
    /// with a negative number, and codes on the wire are unsigned. A program
    /// that closes *with* it says 1011, because from the far side's point of
    /// view that is what happened.
    Overflow,
    /// The socket ended without a close frame, or with a code this enum does
    /// not name. `Overflow`'s note applies to closing with it.
    Abnormal,
}

/// What this server does with a WebSocket: three hooks, and the state they
/// thread between them.
///
/// **`onOpen` decides what a socket carries.** It answers the socket's first
/// state, every later hook gets the current one, and `onMessage` answers the
/// next. So per-socket state is an ordinary value on an ordinary stack, not a
/// table somebody has to key by socket.
///
/// **Hooks on one socket run in order; sockets run concurrently.** One worker
/// owns a socket for its whole life, and the sockets are spread over the same
/// fan-out that answers requests. Any hook may do anything its `C` allows,
/// including waiting.
///
/// `C` is the caller's own context, exactly as `Server.onRequest`'s is, so a
/// hook that pushes on a socket says `C: Sockets` where it is written, and a
/// program whose context lacks it does not compile.
export struct WebSocket<C, S> {
    /// The path this socket is served at. Required, because a server that
    /// upgrades every URL it is asked about has no URLs left for anything
    /// else.
    ///
    /// **An exact match on the path, and nothing else.** The comparison is the
    /// request's `path`, which does not include the query string — so a token
    /// in `?t=…` reaches `onOpen` on the `Request` and nobody has to spell it
    /// here. It is `==` with no normalisation of any kind: `"/socket"` and
    /// `"/socket/"` are two different paths.
    ///
    /// A request to any other path is an ordinary request and reaches
    /// `onRequest`, upgrade headers and all.
    export path: Str,
    /// This server accepted a client's upgrade. Answers the socket's first
    /// state.
    ///
    /// The `Request` is the one that asked — its path, its headers, its query —
    /// and it is where a program reads a subprotocol, a token, or which room
    /// this socket joined.
    export onOpen: fn(C, Socket, Request) => S,
    /// A message arrived. Answers the state the next one will see.
    export onMessage: fn(C, Socket, S, Message) => S,
    /// The socket is gone. Runs once, with the last state, and is the last
    /// thing this socket does.
    ///
    /// **The socket is already closed when this runs**, so anything sent from
    /// here is dropped. Use it to undo what `onOpen` did — stop the actor,
    /// leave the room — not to say goodbye on the wire.
    export onClose: fn(C, Socket, S, CloseReason) => (),
}

impl Socket {
    /// Enqueues a message. **Never waits.**
    ///
    /// The message goes to the socket's outbound buffer and this returns. The
    /// bytes leave when the socket's own worker next pumps it, which for an
    /// idle socket is at once. A buffer that fills closes the socket with
    /// `.Overflow` — see `Server.socketBuffer` — and a message to a socket that
    /// has already closed is dropped.
    ///
    /// `C: Sockets` and nothing more, so the task that pushes here need never
    /// have seen a listener.
    export fn send<C: Sockets>(self, ctx: C, message: Message): () {
        match (message) {
            .Text(text) => ctx.socketSendText(self.0, text),
            .Binary(data) => ctx.socketSendBytes(self.0, data),
        }
    }

    /// Closes the socket, telling the far side why.
    ///
    /// The socket's own worker hears too: its `onClose` runs with the reason
    /// the far side agreed on, which for a close this end started is this one.
    export fn close<C: Sockets>(self, ctx: C, reason: CloseReason): () {
        ctx.socketClose(self.0, codeOf(reason), phraseOf(reason))
    }
}

/// A `CloseReason` as the number the far side gets.
///
/// **`.Overflow` and `.Abnormal` both say 1011**, so the mapping is not a
/// bijection. They are reasons a socket *ended* rather than things one end tells
/// the other, and 1006 — what `.Abnormal` comes back as — may not be sent at all
/// (RFC 6455 §7.4.1).
fn codeOf(reason: CloseReason): Int {
    match (reason) {
        .Normal => 1000,
        .GoingAway => 1001,
        .ProtocolError => 1002,
        .Unsupported => 1003,
        .Policy => 1008,
        .TooLarge => 1009,
        .InternalError => 1011,
        .Overflow => 1011,
        .Abnormal => 1011,
    }
}

/// The words that travel beside the code. Constant per variant, so this costs
/// no context.
fn phraseOf(reason: CloseReason): Str {
    match (reason) {
        .Normal => "closing normally",
        .GoingAway => "going away",
        .ProtocolError => "protocol error",
        .Unsupported => "unsupported data",
        .Policy => "policy violation",
        .TooLarge => "message too large",
        .InternalError => "internal error",
        .Overflow => "the outbound buffer overflowed",
        .Abnormal => "closing abnormally",
    }
}

/// The number a socket closed with, as the reason a program reads.
///
/// **A negative code is the platform's own**, never the far side's. Wire codes
/// are unsigned, so nothing a peer can send reaches this arm: `.Overflow` is
/// something this server knows about its own socket.
///
/// Every other number this enum does not name is `.Abnormal`, including 1005
/// and 1006 — the two RFC 6455 reserves for "there was no code" and "there was
/// no close frame", which is exactly what `.Abnormal` says.
fn reasonOf(code: Int): CloseReason {
    if (code < 0) {
        .Overflow
    } else {
        match (code) {
            1000 => .Normal,
            1001 => .GoingAway,
            1002 => .ProtocolError,
            1003 => .Unsupported,
            1008 => .Policy,
            1009 => .TooLarge,
            1011 => .InternalError,
            _ => .Abnormal,
        }
    }
}
